## Why This Is Asked
Real-time trading platforms demand ultra-low latency combined with robust replay protection across distributed microservices. Token binding mechanisms reduce impersonation risks while maintaining performance requirements critical to trading operations.

## Key Concepts
- Token binding and mutual TLS (mTLS) for connection-specific authentication
- Short time-to-live (TTL) tokens with audience-scoping for service isolation
- WebSocket connection nonces and replay prevention mechanisms
- Global revocation propagation strategies
- Comprehensive observability and audit trail implementation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: validate token and bind to nonce
function authorize(token, nonce, ctx){
  if (!isValidToken(token)) return false;
  if (!bindsToTLS(token, ctx)) return false;
  const session = createSecureSession(token, nonce);
  return session.isValid() && !isReplayAttempt(nonce);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests error handling discipline, prevents data leakage, and demonstrates practical logging and traceability in a real stack.

## Key Concepts

- Centralized error handling with a dedicated AppError class
- Distinguish log content from client response to avoid leakage
- Environment-based verbosity (prod vs dev)
- RequestId for cross-service tracing
- Consistent HTTP status mapping for common errors

## Code Example

```javascript
class AppError extends Error {
  constructor(message, statusCode) {
    super(message)
    this.statusCode = statusCode
    this.isOperational = true
  }
}

function errorHandler(err, req, res, next) {
  const code = err.statusCode || 500
  const isProd = process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production'
  if (isProd) {
    // Do not leak details
    res.status(code).json({ error: 'Something went wrong', code })
  } else {
    // Development: expose details for debugging
    res.status(code).json({ message: err.message, stack: err.stack, code })
  }
  // Log full details securely (stack, req data, requestId) in both modes
}
```

